; 27-type-and-reduce-lambda-terms.kurt
;
; Type and reduce simply typed lambda terms
;
; The lambda theory uses optional sorts to distinguish object terms, object types,
; contexts, and the Boolean judgments Kurt proves.

load lambda

; Typing is written as on paper: `Γ ⊢ M : A`. The complete rules use de Bruijn
; variables internally. `zero` names the newest context entry, so the body below
; has type base in the extended context; abstraction then removes that entry and
; constructs the function type `fun base base`.

(extend empty base) ⊢ (at zero) : base
empty ⊢ (abs base (at zero)) : (fun base base)

; The theory supplies one closed base term, `unit`. Application combines the
; function and argument typing facts.

empty ⊢ unit : base
empty ⊢ (app (abs base (at zero)) unit) : base

; Named terms are convenient when discussing reduction. Binder names do not
; matter: λ x x and λ y y are alpha-equivalent because λ is a real Kurt binder.

empty ⊢ (λ x x) : (fun base base)
empty ⊢ (λ y y) : (fun base base)

; Beta reduction uses capture-avoiding substitution. Here substituting `unit`
; for x in x computes immediately to unit.

(app (λ x x) unit) ↦ unit

; `⇝` is deterministic call-by-value evaluation. It evaluates an application from
; left to right and substitutes only when its argument is already a value.

(app (λ x x) unit) ⇝ unit
value (λ x x)
(app (λ y y) unit) ⇝ unit
(app (λ x x) (app (λ y y) unit)) ⇝ (app (λ x x) unit)

; Sort declarations are ordinary inspection commands too.

list sort lambda
list term lambda
